fn sort_descending(arr: &mut [i32]) {
    let size = arr.len();
    for i in 0..size {
        for j in 0..size - i - 1 {
            if arr[j] < arr[j + 1] {
                let temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this solution, we iterate over the array `arr` using two nested loops. The outer loop (`for i in 0..size`) keeps track of the number of iterations, and the inner loop (`for j in 0..size - i - 1`) compares adjacent elements and swaps them if the current element is smaller than the next element.

By repeatedly swapping elements, the largest element "bubbles" up to the end of the array in each iteration. Hence, after each iteration of the outer loop, the largest element is guaranteed to be in its correct position at the end of the array.

This process continues until the entire array is sorted in descending order.

